---
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Map Timer Technical Writeup | Jack Kroll" description="Technical notes on the Apex Legends Map Timer client, widgets, backend, and caching strategy.">
	<main class="bg-base-100">
		<article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12 md:px-8">
			<a class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm mb-8" href="/projects/maptimer/">Back to Map Timer</a>
			<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
				<span class="badge badge-primary badge-lg">WidgetKit</span>
				<span class="badge badge-secondary badge-lg">Vapor</span>
				<span class="badge badge-accent badge-lg">Cloudflare Tunnels</span>
			</div>
			<h1 class="mt-5 text-4xl font-bold leading-tight md:text-6xl">Map Timer technical writeup</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-xl leading-9 text-base-content/75">
				The core challenge was client and server fusion: keeping map data current without forcing the app or its widgets into constant network traffic.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/jackkroll/MapTimer" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">App source code</a>
				<a class="btn btn-outline" href="https://github.com/jackkroll/MapTimerServer" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Server source code</a>
			</div>

			<div class="prose prose-invert mt-10 max-w-none">
				<h2>Purpose</h2>
				<p>
					Map Timer began as a basic Apex Legends map rotation client. Some maps are less desirable to play, rotations land at different times each day, and checking a website added too much friction. A widget made the information easier to keep nearby.
				</p>

				<h2>Client development</h2>
				<p>
					WidgetKit was a natural fit because standard map rotations are predictable and most of the desired information can be understood at a glance. Widgets can be pre-rendered ahead of time and placed on the home screen or lock screen.
				</p>
				<p>
					The client leans on Apple components where possible to stay aligned with platform design, support light and dark appearances, handle nonstandard display types, and avoid custom UI that ages quickly.
				</p>

				<h2>Server development</h2>
				<p>
					The backend uses Vapor, a Swift server framework, so shared ideas and data structures can stay close to the app code. It is self-hosted on a Raspberry Pi and routed securely through Cloudflare Tunnels.
				</p>

				<h2>Client and server fusion</h2>
				<p>
					The app considered several caching strategies: requesting data every time, polling on an interval, expiring data with a best-by date, and comparing hashes. Hash comparison won because it reduces unnecessary data transfer while still telling the client whether stored schedules match the live schedule.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="mt-10 overflow-x-auto rounded-box border border-base-300 bg-base-200 p-4">
				<div class="min-w-[680px]">
					<ul class="steps steps-vertical lg:steps-horizontal w-full">
						<li class="step step-primary">Request schedule</li>
						<li class="step step-primary">Fetch live hash</li>
						<li class="step step-primary">Compare stored hash</li>
						<li class="step step-primary">Update if stale</li>
						<li class="step step-primary">Return schedule</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
		</article>
	</main>
</Layout>
